A||||6||Fformula88||Metal Floorpan plugs||||1||
Z||000000||Fformula88||10-18-2001||08:30 PM||||A week or two ago we had a thread going about the floorpan plugs that tend to easily rust out and it was believed they are still available from Pontiac.  Well... they are!  I have a few pics and the part numbers for anybody who are interested in ordering them.  The smaller plugs apear to be recently manufactured out of a galvanized metal that appears like it might hold up better than the originals.  They also have rather large rubber "gaskets" I suppose glued to them.<P>Small Plug Part # 2057 9955<BR>Large Plug Part # 1021-5806<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Floorone.JPG">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Floortwo.JPG">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/floorthree.JPG">  <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/floorfour.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by Fformula88 (edited 10-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||10-18-2001||10:34 PM||||So, you finally got them then!<P>Did all years have metal and rubber ones?  My passenger side carpet is pulled out right now and all I can see is metal ones.  Or, does the rubber one go underneath the car somehow?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Master_Sushi||10-18-2001||10:34 PM||||Glad you finally got them!<P>Im still wondering about the rubber plugs. Everybody is talking about the metal ones, but my car has/had rubber ones! If no one knows anything about this maybe ill take one out and snap a pic. I think I left one or two in. They wernt in that well so I can probably pull then through the bottom of the car.LOL<P>All the other holes for them were so rusted out I had to weld in new metal. I'm suprised someone didnt fall through the pasanger floor before I fixed the poor thing!||||reg||5
Z||000003||fierohoho||10-19-2001||05:17 AM||||I also just picked up a set of large and small plugs.<P>I think the plug goes in first and then the gasket is put down over it but I'm not sure what to seal it to the floor and the top of the plug with.<P>Any ideas?<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Banzai||10-19-2001||06:52 AM||||My 84' has the metal plugs covered with the rubber. Weatherstrip adhesive should work well.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||10-19-2001||03:36 PM||||Yep, finally got them in (5 days short of a month  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>I dunno about what came on which years, but I am certain the 85 and up cars had metal.  My dads 85 SE has the metal caps underneath!  If they all had this gasket material on them from the factory is anybody's guess.  I have never seen a Fiero interior with the carpeting pulled up (yet!).  <P>Since its going to be nice this weekend, I'll probably be driving the Fiero instead of working on it, but if I do some work I'll take pics and post a few.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||10-20-2001||08:43 PM||||After everytyhing is cleaned and those are installed, I'd paint over the bottom area with roofing mastic. That should seal them nearly forever. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
